<span class="kicker">2026-08-12 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>How to Install a Brick Paver Walkway on Soil</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>Every walkway eventually meets soil, but not all soil behaves the same way once brick pavers are sitting on top of it. Clay, sandy, and loamy soils each respond differently to compaction, moisture, and seasonal movement, and brick’s higher porosity compared to concrete pavers makes it a bit more sensitive to those differences than other paver materials. Getting a brick walkway to hold up long term means adjusting your approach to the specific soil you’re actually working with, not just following one generic set of steps regardless of what’s underneath.</p>
<p>At Ridgewell Landscape and Design, we install brick walkways across a wide range of soil conditions throughout the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities, and soil type is one of the first things we assess before finalizing a base plan. This guide walks through how clay, sandy, and loamy soils each affect brick installation, along with the full process for building a properly based brick walkway regardless of what soil you’re starting with.</p>
<h2><b>Why Soil Type Matters More for Brick Than for Concrete Pavers</b></h2>
<p>Brick pavers absorb more moisture than dense concrete pavers, which means the soil and base conditions beneath them play a slightly bigger role in long-term performance. Soil that holds water against the base for extended periods, or that expands and contracts significantly with moisture changes, puts more stress on brick’s more porous surface during freeze and thaw cycles than it would on a less absorbent concrete product. This doesn’t mean brick is a poor choice for any particular soil type, but it does mean the base and drainage planning deserve a bit more attention than they might with concrete.</p>
<h2><b>Identifying Your Soil Type</b></h2>
<h3><b>The Simple Squeeze Test</b></h3>
<p>A quick way to get a general sense of your soil type is to take a handful of moist soil and squeeze it into a ball. Sandy soil won’t hold a shape at all and crumbles apart easily. Clay soil holds a tight, dense ball that resists breaking apart even when poked. Loamy soil holds a loose ball shape but breaks apart with light pressure, sitting somewhere between the two extremes.</p>
<h3><b>Checking Drainage Behavior</b></h3>
<p>Beyond texture, it’s worth observing how a section of yard actually handles water. Dig a small test hole, about a foot deep, fill it with water, and watch how long it takes to drain. Water that disappears within a few hours suggests well-draining, sandier soil. Water that lingers for many hours or overnight suggests heavier clay content or poor natural drainage that will need to be accounted for in your base plan.</p>
<h2><b>Installing Brick Pavers on Clay Soil</b></h2>
<h3><b>Why Clay Soil Needs Extra Attention</b></h3>
<p>Clay soil expands when wet and contracts as it dries, and this movement is more pronounced through freeze and thaw cycles than in most other soil types. This seasonal expansion and contraction puts real pressure on whatever is sitting above it, which is why clay-heavy sites, common across parts of the Denver area, need a more robust base to buffer that movement.</p>
<h3><b>Base Depth for Clay Soil</b></h3>
<p>For brick walkways on clay soil, we generally recommend pushing base depth to the full 6 inches of compacted gravel, rather than the lower end of the standard range. This extra depth gives the base more capacity to absorb and distribute the seasonal movement clay soil produces, reducing how much of that movement transfers up into the brick surface itself.</p>
<h3><b>Improving Drainage Beneath the Base</b></h3>
<p>On clay sites, it’s worth considering a layer of larger, more open-graded stone at the very bottom of the excavation, beneath the standard compacted gravel base, to help manage water that would otherwise sit against the base longer than ideal. This is a small addition that meaningfully improves how well the whole system handles moisture in heavier soil.</p>
<h2><b>Installing Brick Pavers on Sandy Soil</b></h2>
<h3><b>Why Sandy Soil Behaves Differently</b></h3>
<p>Sandy soil drains quickly and doesn’t hold moisture the way clay does, which reduces the freeze-thaw pressure that clay soil creates. That said, sandy soil has its own consideration, it can shift more easily under load if not compacted thoroughly, since the individual sand particles have less natural cohesion than clay or loam.</p>
<h3><b>Base Depth for Sandy Soil</b></h3>
<p>A standard 4 inch gravel base is often sufficient on well-draining sandy soil, since the underlying soil isn’t adding significant additional moisture-related movement. That said, thorough compaction still matters just as much here as anywhere else, sandy soil’s looser structure makes proper compaction technique, rather than just base depth, the more important factor.</p>
<h3><b>Watching for Erosion Risk</b></h3>
<p>Sandy soil alongside a brick walkway can be more prone to erosion during heavy rain, since it doesn’t hold together as well as clay or loam. Edge restraints and any surrounding plantings play an important role here in keeping soil from washing away from the walkway’s edges over time.</p>
<h2><b>Installing Brick Pavers on Loamy Soil</b></h2>
<h3><b>Why Loam Is Often the Easiest Starting Point</b></h3>
<p>Loamy soil, a balanced mix of sand, silt, and clay, tends to offer a reasonable middle ground, decent drainage without the extreme moisture retention of heavy clay, and enough cohesion to resist shifting the way pure sand can. Most residential yards fall somewhere in this general range.</p>
<h3><b>Base Depth for Loamy Soil</b></h3>
<p>A base depth of 4 to 5 inches typically works well for loamy soil, matching the general standard range without needing to push toward the upper end the way clay soil requires.</p>

