<h2><b>Soil Type Comparison at a Glance</b></h2>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Soil Type</b></td><td><b>Drainage</b></td><td><b>Base Depth Recommendation</b></td><td><b>Key Consideration</b></td></tr><tr><td>Clay</td><td>Slow</td><td>6 inches</td><td>Freeze-thaw expansion, needs drainage layer</td></tr><tr><td>Sandy</td><td>Fast</td><td>4 inches</td><td>Compaction quality, edge erosion</td></tr><tr><td>Loamy</td><td>Moderate</td><td>4 to 5 inches</td><td>Generally the most forgiving starting point</td></tr></tbody></table><h2><b>What You’ll Need</b></h2>
<p><b>Materials</b></p>
<ul><li>Brick pavers rated for exterior paving use, plus 10 to 15 percent extra for cuts and breakage</li><li>Crushed gravel or road base, quantity based on your soil type’s recommended depth</li><li>Larger open-graded stone for the base of the excavation, if working with clay or poor-draining soil</li><li>Landscape fabric</li><li>Coarse bedding sand</li><li>Polymeric sand for joints</li><li>Edge restraints and spikes</li></ul>
<p><b>Tools</b></p>
<ul><li>Shovel and spade</li><li>A<a href="/blog/choose-the-right-plate-compactor-size-for-a-patio-or-driveway/"> properly sized plate compactor</a></li><li>Rubber mallet</li><li>String line and stakes</li><li>4 foot level</li><li>Wet saw with a masonry blade</li><li>Push broom</li></ul>
<h2><b>Step by Step: Installing Brick Pavers Based on Your Soil</b></h2>
<h3><b>Step 1: Test and Confirm Your Soil Type</b></h3>
<p>Before finalizing your base plan, confirm your soil type using the squeeze test and drainage check described above, since this determines your base depth and whether additional drainage measures are worth including.</p>
<h3><b>Step 2: Excavate to the Appropriate Depth</b></h3>
<p>Excavate to a total depth accounting for your soil-specific base depth, bedding sand, and brick thickness combined. Clay soil sites will excavate slightly deeper than sandy or loamy sites to accommodate the fuller base recommendation.</p>
<h3><b>Step 3: Add a Drainage Layer if Needed</b></h3>
<p>For clay or poor-draining soil, add a layer of larger, open-graded stone at the bottom of the excavation before the standard gravel base, helping water move through rather than sitting against the structure above.</p>
<h3><b>Step 4: Build and Compact the Gravel Base</b></h3>
<p>Add crushed gravel in 2 inch layers, compacting thoroughly with a plate compactor after each layer, working toward your soil-specific target depth.</p>
<h3><b>Step 5: Add Landscape Fabric and Bedding Sand</b></h3>
<p>Roll landscape fabric over the compacted base, then add and screed about 1 inch of coarse sand to create a level surface for the brick.</p>
<h3><b>Step 6: Lay the Brick Following Your Pattern</b></h3>
<p>Place brick pavers snugly against each other, tapping each into position with a rubber mallet. Our guide on<a href="/blog/how-to-lay-a-paver-walkway/"> how to lay a paver walkway</a> covers pattern options that work well with brick’s smaller format.</p>
<h3><b>Step 7: Cut With a Wet Saw</b></h3>
<p>Cut brick with a wet saw designed for masonry rather than a dry angle grinder, since brick is more prone to unpredictable cracking with a dry cutting approach.</p>
<h3><b>Step 8: Install Edge Restraints</b></h3>
<p>Secure edge restraints tightly against the outer edge of the brick field, anchoring with spikes. This matters especially on sandy soil, where edge erosion is a bigger concern.</p>
<h3><b>Step 9: Fill Joints With Polymeric Sand</b></h3>
<p>Sweep polymeric sand into the joints, remove excess from the surface, and mist to activate.</p>
<h3><b>Step 10: Final Compaction</b></h3>
<p>Run the plate compactor over the finished walkway one final time to set everything firmly in place.</p>
<h2><b>Sealing Considerations Based on Soil Type</b></h2>
<p>Because brick absorbs more moisture than concrete, sealing is worth stronger consideration across all soil types, but especially on clay soil, where the base underneath is managing more moisture movement overall. Reapplying sealer every two to three years helps reduce water absorption and protects brick from surface flaking during freeze and thaw cycles, regardless of which soil type your walkway sits on. Our broader guide on<a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-brick-paver-walkway/"> building a brick paver walkway</a> covers sealing recommendations in more detail.</p>
<h2><b>Common Mistakes When Installing Brick on Different Soils</b></h2>
<ul><li>Using the same base depth regardless of soil type instead of adjusting for clay’s added moisture movement</li><li>Skipping a drainage layer on clay or poor-draining sites</li><li>Under-compacting sandy soil, assuming its looser structure needs less compaction effort</li><li>Not accounting for erosion risk on sandy soil when planning edge restraints and surrounding plantings</li><li>Assuming loamy soil needs no special consideration at all, when proper compaction still matters regardless</li></ul>
<h2><b>Why Soil-Specific Planning Is Worth the Extra Step</b></h2>
<p>A brick walkway built with a one-size-fits-all base approach might hold up fine on forgiving loamy soil, but the same approach on heavy clay or loose sandy soil is far more likely to develop problems within the first few years. Taking the time to identify your actual soil type and adjust base depth and drainage accordingly is a relatively small additional step that meaningfully improves how long the finished walkway lasts.</p>
<p>With over 15 years of installing brick walkways across a wide range of soil conditions throughout the Denver area, our team at Ridgewell Landscape and Design has a strong sense of how to adjust a base plan for whatever soil a specific property actually has. Explore our<a href="/services/paver-patios/"> paver patio services</a> to see the kind of properly based brick work we’ve built across different soil conditions.</p>

<h2><b>Frequently Asked Questions</b></h2>
<h3><b>How do I know what type of soil I have before installing brick pavers?</b></h3>
<p>A simple squeeze test, forming moist soil into a ball, along with a drainage test using a water-filled hole, gives a good general sense of whether you’re working with clay, sandy, or loamy soil.</p>
<h3><b>Does clay soil really need a deeper base for brick pavers?</b></h3>
<p>Yes. Clay soil’s significant expansion and contraction through freeze and thaw cycles puts more pressure on the base, so a fuller 6 inch depth helps absorb that movement rather than transferring it into the brick surface.</p>
<h3><b>Is sandy soil easier to work with for a brick walkway?</b></h3>
<p>In terms of drainage and freeze-thaw movement, yes, but sandy soil requires thorough compaction and extra attention to edge erosion, since it doesn’t hold together as well as clay or loam.</p>
<h3><b>Should I seal brick pavers differently based on soil type?</b></h3>
<p>Sealing is beneficial across all soil types given brick’s porosity, but it’s especially worth prioritizing on clay soil, where the base is managing more overall moisture movement.</p>
<h3><b>What’s the easiest soil type to install a brick walkway on?</b></h3>
<p>Loamy soil generally offers the most forgiving starting point, with moderate drainage and enough cohesion to resist the shifting sandy soil can experience, without clay’s significant freeze-thaw expansion.</p>

<h2><b>Ready to Install a Brick Walkway Built for Your Soil</b></h2>
<p>Getting the base right for your specific soil type is one of the most important factors in how long a brick walkway holds up, and it’s not something a generic approach handles well across every yard.</p>
<p>Ridgewell Landscape and Design has been installing brick walkways across a wide range of soil conditions in Denver, Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities for over 15 years. If you’d like your soil assessed and your walkway built accordingly,<a href="/contact/"> reach out for a free quote and consultation</a>.</p>

